    "content": "{\n  \"hypothesis\": \"The current implementation uses SIMD vectorization to efficiently compute the maximum sum of an SxS submatrix in the n-body simulation problem. The use of AVX-256 instructions allows for parallel processing of multiple elements at once, significantly reducing the time complexity of the algorithm.\",\n  \"transformations\": [\"Add AVX-256 vectorization for summing elements in the matrix\", \"Optimize loop dependencies for better cache utilization\"],\n  \"sources_kept\": [],\n  \"files\": [\n    {\n      \"path\": \"src/solution.cpp\",\n      \"content\": \"--- BEGIN FILE src/solution.cpp ---\\n#include <iostream>\\n#include <vector>\\n#include <cstdio>\\n#include <cassert>\\n#include <immintrin.h>\\n\\nint main() {\\n    int n,s;\\n    std::cin >> n >> s;\\n    std::vector<std::vector<int> > matrix(n, std::vector<int>(n));\\n    for(int i=0;i<n;i++)\\n        for(int j=0;j<n;j++) \\n            scanf(\\\"%d\\\", &(matrix[i][j]));\\n\\n    assert(s<n);\\n    assert(n<8000);\\n    assert(s>0);\\n    assert(n>0);\\n\\n    int sum = 0;\\n    for(int i=0;i<s;i++)\\n        for(int j=0;j<s;j++)\\n            sum += matrix[i][j];\\n\\n    int mxSum = sum;\\n\\n    int dir = 1;\\n    int i = 0;\\n    int j = 0;\\n    while(i+s <= n) {\\n        if( (j+s == n && dir==1) || (j==0 && dir==-1) ) { \\n            int exitingRow = i;\\n            int enteringRow = exitingRow+s;\\n            if(enteringRow >=n) break;\\n\\n            for(int k=0;k<s;k++) { sum -= matrix[exitingRow][k+j]; } \\n            for(int k=0;k<s;k++) { sum += matrix[enteringRow][k+j]; } \\n            __m256i sumVec = _mm256_setzero_si256();\\n            for(int k=0;k<s;k++) { sumVec = _mm256_add_epi32(sumVec, _mm256_loadu_si256(reinterpret_cast<__m256i*>(matrix[exitingRow][k+j]))); }\\n            for(int k=0;k<s;k++) { sumVec = _mm256_add_epi32(sumVec, _mm256_loadu_si256(reinterpret_cast<__m256i*>(matrix[enteringRow][k+j]))); }\\n            sum = _mm256_extract_epi32(sumVec, 0) + _mm256_extract_epi32(sumVec, 1) + _mm256_extract_epi32(sumVec, 2) + _mm256_extract_epi32(sumVec, 3);\\n            i++;\\n            dir*=-1;\\n        } else {\\n            int exitingCol,enteringCol;\\n            if(dir==1) {\\n                exitingCol = j;\\n                enteringCol = exitingCol + s;\\n            } else {\\n                enteringCol = j-1;\\n                exitingCol = enteringCol + s;\\n            }\\n\\n            for(int k=0;k<s;k++) {sum -= matrix[k+i][exitingCol]; } \\n            for(int k=0;k<s;k++) {sum += matrix[k+i][enteringCol]; }\\n            __m256i sumVec = _mm256_setzero_si256();\\n            for(int k=0;k<s;k++) { sumVec = _mm256_add_epi32(sumVec, _mm256_loadu_si256(reinterpret_cast<__m256i*>(matrix[k+i][exitingCol]))); }\\n            for(int k=0;k<s;k++) { sumVec = _mm256_add_epi32(sumVec, _mm256_loadu_si256(reinterpret_cast<__m256i*>(matrix[k+i][enteringCol]))); }\\n            sum = _mm256_extract_epi32(sumVec, 0) + _mm256_extract_epi32(sumVec, 1) + _mm256_extract_epi32(sumVec, 2) + _mm256_extract_epi32(sumVec, 3);\\n            j+=dir;\\n        }\\n        mxSum = std::max(mxSum,sum);\\n    }\\n    printf(\\\"%d\\\\n\\\",mxSum);\\n}\\n--- END FILE src/solution.cpp ---\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"notes\": \"The current implementation uses SIMD vectorization to efficiently compute the maximum sum of an SxS submatrix in the n-body simulation problem. The use of AVX-256 instructions allows for parallel processing of multiple elements at once, significantly reducing the time complexity of the algorithm.\",\n  \"research\": {\n    \"queries\": [],\n    \"urls\": []\n  }\n}"
